lesson: 130
title: A staircase of cubes and handshakes
level: primary-1
stars: 3
explain: A staircase of 4 steps built of cubes has 1, 2, 3 and 4 cubes in its steps: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 cubes.
explain: The same sum counts handshakes: of 5 children, the first shakes hands with 4 others, the next with 3 new ones, then 2, then 1, so 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10.
explain: Pair the numbers from both ends to add fast: 1 + 4 = 5 and 2 + 3 = 5.
vars: a=name n=3..8
answer: n * (n + 1) / 2
exercise: {a} builds a staircase of cubes with {n} steps. The lowest step is 1 cube high, and each next step is 1 cube higher. How many cubes does {a.he} need?
exercise: Coins are laid in a triangle: 1 coin in the top row, 2 in the next, 3 in the next, and so on, with {n} rows. How many coins are there?
exercise: {n+1} children meet, and each child shakes hands with every other child once. How many handshakes are there?
exercise: In a tournament {n+1} teams each play every other team once. How many games are played?
exercise: Add up all the numbers from 1 to {n}.
test: At a party {n+1} friends each clink glasses with every other friend once. How many clinks are there?
test: There are {n+1} towns, and a road joins every two of them. How many roads are there?
test: The steps of a staircase are made of blocks: the shortest step is 1 block, and each next step is 1 block taller, up to {n} blocks. How many blocks are there in all?
test: What does {a} get when {a.he} adds every whole number from 1 up to and including {n}?
exercise: {a} builds stairs from cubes. There are {n} steps: the first is 1 cube high, the next 2, and so on. How many cubes are used in all?
exercise: Coins make a triangle: the top row has 1 coin and each row below has 1 more, for {n} rows. How many coins are in the triangle altogether?
exercise: Every one of {n+1} children at a party in Ohio shakes hands with every other child once. How many handshakes happen?
exercise: {n+1} teams in a football league in Ireland each play each other once. How many matches are there?
exercise: Find the total of 1 + 2 + 3 + … + {n}.
exercise: Tins are stacked in a pyramid at a shop in Sydney: 1 tin on top, 2 below it, 3 below that, down to {n} on the bottom. How many tins are there?
exercise: What is the sum of every whole number from 1 to {n}?
exercise: Each pair of {n+1} children plays one game of chess. How many games are played?
test: At a wedding in Dublin, {n+1} guests clink glasses, each pair exactly once. How many clinks are there?
test: {n+1} villages in Wales are each joined to every other village by one footpath. How many footpaths are there?
test: Bowling pins are set in rows: 1, then 2, then 3, and so on, up to a row of {n}. How many pins are there in all?
test: How many games are played when {n+1} players in a tennis club in Melbourne each play every other player once?
test: {a} adds 1, 2, 3 and every number up to {n}. What total does {a.he} get?
test: Every two of {n+1} points on a circle are joined by a straight line. How many lines are drawn?
